== 3...Nf6 · Closed Tarrasch ==

<blockquote>3...Nf6 is the most tactical option for black. He doesn't play for a solid position but prefers to complicate matters with dynamic play. Very interesting positions arise, with rich play and fascinating ideas for both sides.<br>―IM Andreas Tzermiadianos<ref>{{Cite book |title=How to Beat the French Defence: The Essential Guide to the Tarrasch |last=Tzermiadianos |first=Andreas |publisher=Everyman Chess |year=2008 |isbn=9781857445671 |chapter=13}}</ref></blockquote>

For those Black players who don't want to play with an isolated d-pawn (at least: not yet), the 3....Nf6 variation is a good
alternative to 3....c5.

Black has two attackers on e4 to White's one defender, and even if White adds another (e.g. 4. f3?), 4...dxe4 will simultaneously open up another attack on White's undefended d pawn. 

Therefore [[/4. e5|'''4. e5''']] is essentially the only move. This closes up the position, takes space and forces Black to move their knight again. 

'''4. Bd3''' is playable, but transposes after 4...c5 5. e5. Otherwise 5. c3!? cxd4 6. cxd4 dxe4 7. Nxe4 Nxe4 8. Bxe4 Bb4+ equalises for Black. 

